      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We can rewrite the expression as\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{x^4}{x^8 + 2x^6 - 4x^4 + 8x^2 + 16} &= \\dfrac{x^4}{(x^4 + 2x^2 - 4)^2 + 12x^2} \\\\\n&= \\dfrac{x^4}{(x^2 + 1)^2(x^2 - 4)^2 + 12x^2}.\n\\end{align*}To maximize this expression, we want the top to be maximized relative to the bottom.\nSo, we want to minimize the fraction $\\dfrac{1}{(x^2 + 1)^2(x^2 - 4)^2 + 12x^2} = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 + 12x^2},$\nwhere $r = x^2 + 1.$\nSince $x^2 \\ge 0$ for all real numbers $x,$ we know that the denominator can be equal to zero only when $x=0.$\nUsing the quadratic formula, we find that\n\\begin{align*}\nr^2 + 12x^2 &= (x^2 + 1)^2 \\\\\n&= (x^2 - 2x + 1)^2 + 12x^2 \\\\\n&= (x^2 - 4)^2 + 12x^2 \\\\\n&= (x^2 + 4)^2 - 8x^2 \\\\\n&= (x^2 - 4)^2 + 12x^2,\n\\end{align*}where we evaluated the expression at $x = 0,$ so we can conclude that $r>0.$\n\nSo, the minimum value of the numerator $\\dfrac{1}{r^2 + 12x^2}$ is $\\dfrac{1}{4},$ which occurs when $x=\\pm 2$. Since $x^2 \\ge 0$ and $(x^2 + 1)^2 > 0$ for all real numbers, the expression is maximized when $x= \\pm 2.$ So, we have $\\dfrac{x^4}{x^8 + 2x^6 - 4x^4 + 8x^2 + 16} \\le \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{4}}.$QED. The answer is \\frac{1}{4}",
