      "text": "We start by simplifying the expression by dividing both the numerator and denominator by $x^4.$ This results in:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{x^4}{x^8 + 2x^6 - 4x^4 + 8x^2 + 16} &= \\frac{1}{x^4 + \\frac{2}{x^2} - 4 + \\frac{8}{x^2} + \\frac{16}{x^4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{(x^2 + \\frac{2}{x^2})^2 - 4}.\n\\end{align*}\nNow, we have a new expression as a sum of squares, the second \nterm being negative. The expression $\\left(x^2 + \\frac{2}{x^2}\\right)^2 - 4$ \nmust be nonnegative, so as long as\n\\[x^2 + \\frac{2}{x^2} \\neq 2,\\]\nthe new expression is nonnegative. Considering cases, we see\n$$\\left(x^2 + \\frac{2}{x^2}\\right)^2 - 4 \\ge 0 \\Longleftrightarrow x^2 + \\frac{2}{x^2} \\ge 2 > 0.$$By the AM-GM inequality, $\\frac{x^2 + \\frac{2}{x^2}}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{\\frac{x^2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{x^2}}{2}} = \\sqrt{1} = 1.$\nThus,\n\\begin{align*}\n(x^2 + \\frac{2}{x^2})^2 - 4 &\\ge 1^2 - 4 \\\\\n&= -3.\n\\end{align*}\nSo $\\frac{x^4}{x^8 + 2x^6 - 4x^4 + 8x^2 + 16}$ is minimized at $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{-3}}$.\nThe answer is: \\frac{1}{-3}",
