      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let $a = x^2.$  Then the expression equals\n\\[\\frac{a^2}{a^4 + 2a^3 - 4a^2 + 8a + 16}.\\]We want to maximize the value of this expression.  Note that\n\\begin{align*}\n f(a) &= a^4 + 2a^3 - 4a^2 + 8a + 16 \\\\\n &= (a^4 + 2a^3 - 4a^2 - 8a + 16) + (4a^3 + 4a + 16) \\\\\n &= (a^4 + 2a^3 - 4a^2 - 8a + 16) + 4(a^3 + a + 4) \\\\\n &= (a^4 + 2a^3 - 4a^2 - 8a + (a^3 + a + 4)) + 16 \\\\\n &= (a-2)^2 (a+2)^2 + 16.\n\\end{align*}This quadratic is always positive, so for any real number $a,$ $f(a) \\ge 16.$  Moreover, if $f(x) = 16,$ then\n\\begin{align*}\n a &= x^2 \\\\\n &= (-2)^2 \\\\\n &= 4.\n\\end{align*}Thus, when $x = -2,$ the original expression takes on a value of $\\frac{16}{16} = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value is $\\boxed{1}.$. The answer is 1",
